<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leodegar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 615–679</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Leger, Leodegar of Autun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leodegar, known in French as Saint Léger, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a> in Merovingian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and a leading opponent of the mayor of the palace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebroin">Ebroin</a>. After years of conflict he was blinded, deposed and put to death in 679, and was soon venerated as a martyr. He became the patron saint of Lucerne, whose monastery and later collegiate church, the Hofkirche, are dedicated to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leodegar.jpg" alt="Leodegar" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Loïc LLH · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%A9tail_vitraux_du_choeur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
